    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we can complete the square for the terms in \\( x \\) and \\( y \\).\n\nStart by focusing on the \\( x \\)-terms:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x \\]\nTo complete the square, subtract and add a constant that makes this expression a perfect square trinomial:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x + 9 - 9 = (x+3)^2 - 9 \\]\n\nNow, look at the \\( y \\)-terms:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y \\]\nAgain, to complete the square, subtract and add a constant that makes this expression a perfect square trinomial for \\( y \\):\n\\[ 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y-1)^2 - 1) = 2(y-1)^2 - 2 \\]\n\nPutting it all together:\n\\[ z = (x+3)^2 - 9 + 2(y-1)^2 - 2 + 22 \\]\n\\[ z = (x+3)^2 + 2(y-1)^2 + 11 \\]\n\nThis expression is minimized when both squares \\((x+3)^2\\) and \\(2(y-1)^2\\) are minimized. Since they are squares of real numbers, they are always non-negative. Hence, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is:\n\\[ (x+3)^2 + 2(y-1)^2 = 0 \\]\nSo the minimum value of \\( z \\) is:\n\\[ 11 \\]\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
